## IV. The institutions

### purdue-pharma
- The launch, 1996. Richard Sackler, then senior vice president of sales, told the launch party that OxyContin would be "followed by a blizzard of prescriptions that will bury the competition".
- The marketing. The FDA allowed Purdue to claim that the delayed-absorption formula reduced abuse liability, with no scientific evidence for it. Sales representatives told doctors the risk of addiction was "less than one percent". Purdue made 14,000 copies of a video saying opioids caused addiction in fewer than 1% of patients, a claim FDA officials later called unsubstantiated.
- The growth. Prescriptions rose from 316,000 in 1996 to more than 14 million in 2001 and 2002, and annual sales passed $1 billion soon after Richard Sackler became CEO in 1999.
- The first plea, 2007. Purdue pleaded guilty to felony "misbranding" of OxyContin "with the intent to defraud or mislead" and paid about $600 million. Three executives pleaded guilty to misdemeanours and paid $34 million between them.
- After the plea. Massachusetts' 2019 complaint alleges the family went on misleading doctors and patients. A second federal plea in 2020 was to felony charges, including kickbacks to doctors and to an electronic-health-records company to drive prescriptions.
- The settlement: $7.4 billion, about $6.5 billion from the Sacklers and $900 million from Purdue, paid over 15 years. 55 states and territories agreed in June 2025, after the Supreme Court in June 2024 overturned the earlier plan, which shielded the Sacklers from liability.
- Judge Sean Lane confirmed the plan in November 2025. The Sacklers give up ownership of Purdue, and the first payments were expected in early 2026.
**links:** oxycodone · mckinsey · hyperactivism (Avik's indictment of the medical system) · workflow goals purdue-pharma and oxycontin.

### mckinsey
It paid $650 million (December 2024, Department of Justice) over its work for Purdue, which included advice to "turbocharge" OxyContin sales.
**links:** purdue-pharma · workflow goal mckinsey.

### distributors-and-pharmacies
- **The distributors.** McKesson ($7.9 billion), Cardinal Health ($6.4 billion) and AmerisourceBergen ($6.4 billion) settled in 2021 for up to $21 billion over 18 years. Johnson & Johnson (Janssen) added up to $5 billion. The settlement created a clearinghouse in which each distributor must account for the others' shipments to detect suspicious orders.
- **The pharmacies.** CVS (about $5 billion over 10 years), Walgreens (about $5.7 billion over 15 years) and Walmart (about $3.1 billion) settled in 2022, as did the manufacturers Teva and Allergan.
- Together the opioid settlements pass $50 billion, most of it required to go to abatement. None admitted wrongdoing.
- *Claude's gloss:* the supply chain had the order data the whole time. The clearinghouse is a knowledge graph of shipments, built only under a court settlement.
**links:** purdue-pharma · mckinsey · oxycodone · the-information-gap.

### settlement-money (where it goes)
- Companies accused of reckless marketing and distribution are paying roughly $58 billion in opioid settlements. Most of it is meant for abatement, and states must spend at least 70% on remediation.
- KFF Health News, with Johns Hopkins and Shatterproof, built the largest database of the spending (more than 10,500 decisions). It found more than $61 million in 2024 went to law-enforcement-related uses, including Tasers, cruisers, night-vision gear, rifle suppressors in Indiana and drones in Arizona. Other money went to unproven prevention and to filling local budget gaps.
- Examples of misuse: $600,000 on opioid-awareness concerts in New Jersey, called "waste" and "misuse" by the state comptroller. Nassau County, New York, moved nearly $14 million of interest earned on unspent settlement money into its general fund.
- Minnesota is one of 20 states committed to reporting all of its spending.
- *Claude's gloss:* the money meant to close the evidence–practice gap is being spent without the evidence. Contingency management and medication treatment are the proven uses, and the information needed to steer the money is the information that is missing.
**links:** purdue-pharma · distributors-and-pharmacies · methadone and buprenorphine · contingency-management · hyperactivism.

### war-on-drugs
- Nixon declared drug abuse "public enemy number one" on 17 June 1971, after signing the Controlled Substances Act in 1970.
- In 1994 his domestic-policy chief, John Ehrlichman, told Dan Baum (published in Harper's, 2016) that the Nixon White House had "two enemies: the antiwar left and black people". The quote's validity is disputed by Ehrlichman's family and some historians.
- The US prison population grew from about 300,000 to 2.3 million after 1971. The 1986 crack/powder 100:1 ratio is under cocaine.
**links:** prohibition · cocaine · cannabis · lsd · keltner (compassion as the inverse policy).

### china-precursors (the supply chain)
- The DEA first named China as the main source of US-bound fentanyl and precursors in 2015; Chinese-sourced fentanyl was 97% of the high-purity fentanyl seized from shipments to the US in 2016–2017. After China scheduled fentanyl-related substances as a class in 2019, production moved to Mexican cartels (Sinaloa, Jalisco) using Chinese, and increasingly Indian, precursor chemicals and pill presses (US Annual Threat Assessment, March 2026).
- In November 2025 China's Ministry of Commerce placed export controls on 13 precursor chemicals bound for North America. On 15 December 2025 Executive Order 14367 designated "illicit fentanyl and its core precursor chemicals" weapons of mass destruction, directing defence and homeland-security agencies to fold fentanyl into counter-WMD planning and the Attorney General to intensify prosecutions. Legal commentators noted that an executive order cannot change the statutory definition. In February 2026 the Supreme Court struck down tariffs imposed under IEEPA, including the "fentanyl" tariffs on Chinese goods (CRS IF10890, October 2026).
- DEA seizure data show fentanyl purity falling sharply through 2025 (Filter). Fentanyl was involved in about 42,000 deaths from April 2024 to April 2025, against more than 70,000 at the peak.
- *Claude's gloss:* the supply-side account of the decline is now the strongest single candidate, and it predicts its own failure mode: a weaker or scarcer opioid supply is replaced by whatever is cheap to make (nitazenes, medetomidine, stimulants), as every substitution in this document shows. Dilution saves lives only while the people who use drugs still have tolerance matched to it; a later return to purity would meet a population with less tolerance. The evidence-practice gap, not the supply, is the variable under policy control.
**links:** fentanyl · supply-substitution · the-decline · global · boat-strikes · prohibition.

### boat-strikes (the war on drugs as war)
- From September 2025 the US military, in Operation Southern Spear, has struck small boats it says are carrying drugs, first off Venezuela's Caribbean coast and from October in the eastern Pacific. By March 2026 a defence official put the toll at 157 people in 45 strikes; by June 2026 AP counted more than 200 dead in more than 60 boats. A follow-up strike on the first boat killed its survivors. The campaign was part of the military buildup against Venezuela that ended in Nicolás Maduro's capture.
- **The administration's case:** the US is in "armed conflict" with cartels designated as terrorist organisations; each boat destroyed saves American lives (the President said 25,000 per boat); law enforcement alone has not stopped the flow.
- **The critics' case:** the boats in these waters carry cocaine, not fentanyl, which comes overland from Mexico; the military has offered little evidence that the people killed were traffickers; killing suspects without arrest or trial is extrajudicial, AP identified four of the dead in a Venezuelan town, and a lawsuit over the strikes calls them murders ordered at the highest levels of government; members of Congress dispute that any authorisation covers them. The Coast Guard's conventional interdiction seizes cocaine and arrests crews.
- *Claude's gloss:* on its own premises (cartels as combatants, drugs as weapons) the policy is coherent. The premises do not hold for the facts: the drug killing Americans is not on those boats, and a fisherman or a courier is not a combatant. It is the Philippines' logic exported: the endpoint of treating drug use as an enemy is killing people who are not shown to have done anything. It also leaves every death in this document untouched, because none of them is caused at sea.
**links:** war-on-drugs · philippines · cocaine · fentanyl · china-precursors · prohibition.

### prohibition (as a mechanism)
*Claude's gloss, built from the nodes above:* prohibition does four things that kill.
- It removes information from the supply: unknown dose, unknown molecule.
- It pushes the supply to more potent, more compact molecules, because they are easier to smuggle: opium, then heroin, then fentanyl, then nitazenes.
- It blocks medicine: Schedule I research barriers, an ibogaine ban since the 1960s.
- It turns treatment into a crime, through policing and the anti-kickback cap on contingency management.
**links:** every substance node · drug-checking · contingency-management.

### dea-quotas
The DEA sets aggregate production quotas for Schedule II drugs, and the Adderall shortage followed from those quotas.
**links:** adderall · prohibition.

