## V. What works, and how little it is used

### methadone and buprenorphine (medications for opioid use disorder)
- Among overdose survivors in Massachusetts (Larochelle et al., Annals of Internal Medicine, 2018), methadone cut mortality 59% and buprenorphine 38%.
- In 2022 only 25.1% of US adults who needed opioid treatment received these medications (CDC MMWR, 2024).
- NIDA's Nora Volkow: we now have effective treatments, "yet tens of thousands of people die each year because they have not received these treatments".
**links:** μ-opioid · evidence–practice gap · purdue-pharma (settlement funds earmarked for treatment).

### x-waiver (the buprenorphine licence that was removed)
- From 2000 (the Drug Addiction Treatment Act) a clinician needed a separate DEA "X-waiver", with training and a patient cap (up to 275), to prescribe buprenorphine for addiction. No such waiver was needed to prescribe oxycodone. The Mainstreaming Addiction Treatment Act, passed in the December 2022 budget bill, eliminated it on 12 January 2023: any clinician licensed for controlled substances can now prescribe it.
- Chua et al. (NEJM, 2024, IQVIA prescription data): prescribers rose to more than 53,600 by December 2023, 11,500 more than a year earlier. Patients on buprenorphine stayed near 810,000–830,000 a month, and new starts rose only from 46,500 to 48,200 a month.
- A TriNetX record study of about a million adults with an OUD diagnosis found buprenorphine prescribed after 18.8% of new diagnoses before the change and 19.6% after.
- *Claude's gloss:* the waiver was a barrier, but not the binding one. Removing it raised supply of prescribers without raising treatment. The remaining constraints sit elsewhere: pharmacies that do not stock it (DEA suspicious-order monitoring after the distributor settlements), prior authorisation, stigma among clinicians, and patients never offered it. One gate removed shows where the others are.
**links:** methadone and buprenorphine · distributors-and-pharmacies · dea-quotas · racial-disparities.

### methadone-regulation (the clinic model)
- In the US methadone for addiction can only be dispensed by an opioid treatment program (OTP): a federally certified clinic, historically with daily observed dosing. In Canada, Australia, the UK, France, Germany, Switzerland and the Netherlands community pharmacies dispense it; in Australia most of 47,500 methadone patients (2021) received it at a pharmacy, and in the UK any prescriber can write it.
- SAMHSA's final rule revising 42 CFR Part 8 (published 2 February 2024, effective 2 April 2024, compliance 2 October 2024), the first update in over 20 years: it drops the requirement of a year of active addiction before admission, makes counselling optional rather than a condition of medication, makes permanent the COVID-era take-home doses (scaled to time in treatment, up to 28 days after a month) and allows telehealth starts for buprenorphine (not for methadone). Take-home methadone during COVID was associated with similar or better outcomes.
- The Modernizing Opioid Treatment Access Act (bipartisan, 2023) would let addiction-medicine physicians prescribe methadone for pharmacy dispensing. It has not passed. The OTP trade association opposes it.
- *Claude's gloss:* methadone cuts mortality by about 60% (Larochelle), and the US is the only peer country that confines it to clinics. The clinic model was built in 1972 to control the patient, not to treat them; daily attendance is a cost paid in jobs, travel and dignity, and the patients who cannot pay it die at the rate of the untreated. The 2024 rule moved the constraint; MOTAA would remove it, and the industry whose business is the constraint opposes its removal.
**links:** methadone and buprenorphine · x-waiver · heroin-assisted-treatment · racial-disparities (clinics in Black and Hispanic neighbourhoods).

### racial-disparities
- From 2010 to 2019 Black Americans went from least likely to die of an opioid overdose to more likely than white Americans. Since 2020 Black communities have had the second-highest overdose death rate after Native Americans. Between 2021 and 2022 the Black rate rose 7% while the white rate fell. Black people have the highest cocaine-involved overdose death rates, though they do not use cocaine at higher rates.
- In Medicare claims (Harvard and Dartmouth, 2023), buprenorphine was received after 12.7% of opioid-related events among Black patients, 18.7% among Hispanic patients and 23.3% among white patients. The patients had similar numbers of healthcare visits, so the gap was not explained by less contact with care.
- Per the CDC, only 1 in 12 Black people who died of an opioid overdose had been in treatment, about half the rate for white people.
- Methadone clinics are concentrated in low-income, Black and Hispanic neighbourhoods and take Medicaid; buprenorphine prescribers concentrate in white neighbourhoods. Methadone requires near-daily clinic visits; buprenorphine is prescribed from an office.
- *Claude's gloss:* the first wave (prescription opioids) was marketed into white and rural America, and the treatment built for it followed the same lines. The war on drugs' 100:1 crack ratio and the treatment gap are one pattern: punishment for some, medicine for others.
**links:** methadone and buprenorphine · war-on-drugs · cocaine · purdue-pharma.

### native-nations (the highest death rate of any group)
- American Indian and Alaska Native people have the highest age-adjusted overdose death rate in the US in both 2023 and 2024 (CDC NCHS Data Brief 549, January 2026). In 2023 it was 65.0 per 100,000, against 5.1 for Asian Americans, a ratio above 12 (Healthy People 2030). From 2021 to 2022 it rose 15%, from 56.6 to 65.2, the sharpest increase of any group. In 2024 their opioid death rate was 35.5 per 100,000, the highest of any group (KFF). In Alaska the AI/AN rate was 101.9 per 100,000 in 2024 (114.6 in 2023).
- Alcohol-induced death rates are also highest among AI/AN people: 61.5 per 100,000 in 2023 (TFAH, Pain in the Nation 2025).
- The Indian Health Service, the main health system for about 2.6 million American Indians and Alaska Natives, has been funded far below its own requested need for decades.
- In February 2022 tribes reached a $590 million settlement with Johnson & Johnson (Janssen: $150 million over two years) and the three largest distributors (McKesson, AmerisourceBergen, Cardinal Health: about $440 million over seven years). With the distributors' earlier $75 million deal with the Cherokee Nation the total was $665 million. All 574 federally recognised tribes may take part, whether or not they sued; about 15% goes to lawyers, and the rest to treatment and abatement under tribal control. It recognised the tribes as a distinct litigating body, separate from the states' $26 billion deal. J&J admitted no wrongdoing and called its promotion of opioids "appropriate and responsible".
- *Claude's gloss:* the groups dying fastest are those whose communities were first dispossessed and then given the least-funded health system in the country. The settlement is the first in which the harm was paid to the nations themselves. Settlement money spent under tribal authority on culturally rooted treatment connects to sacred use (the Native American Church's peyote ceremony has long been used in recovery from alcohol) and to Rat Park's finding that the environment is a cause.
**links:** racial-disparities · distributors-and-pharmacies · settlement-money · sacred use (peyote) · alcohol and tobacco · rat-park.

### contingency-management
- Contingency management means small cash or prize rewards for negative tests. It is the only treatment consistently associated with reduced stimulant use, it has been shown effective in more than 100 studies, and fewer than 10% of treatment programs offer it.
- Federal anti-kickback rules (incentives capped at $75 per patient) and fear of fraud liability restrict it. California's CalAIM pilot offers it under Medi-Cal.
- *Claude's gloss:* it uses the reward system the drug hijacked to retrain it. A rule written against fraud by doctors caps the best treatment for the fastest-growing cause of overdose death.
**links:** dopamine · methamphetamine · cocaine · prohibition.

### stimulant-medications (none approved)
- No medication is FDA-approved for methamphetamine or cocaine use disorder. Deaths involving psychostimulants doubled between the twelve months ending October 2019 and October 2021 (NIDA). Methamphetamine overdose deaths rose fivefold from 2012 to 2018. Among patients with opioid use disorder, past-month methamphetamine use rose from 19% in 2011 to 34% in 2017 ("twin epidemics").
- ADAPT-2 (Trivedi et al., NEJM, January 2021; NIDA Clinical Trials Network; 403 participants in stage 1 and 225 in stage 2): extended-release injectable naltrexone (380 mg every three weeks) with extended-release oral bupropion produced a response in 13.6% against 2.5% on placebo, a small absolute effect that NIDA called a breakthrough. A secondary analysis (Li et al., Addiction, 2024) found methamphetamine-negative urine tests rose 27% on the combination against 11% on placebo.
- The supply changed: Mexican producers moved from the pseudoephedrine route (restricted by the 2005 Combat Methamphetamine Epidemic Act) to the P2P route, producing cheaper, purer methamphetamine at scale. Clinicians and researchers (notably Sam Quinones, The Least of Us, 2021) link P2P methamphetamine to more severe psychosis; that link is reported but not yet established in controlled studies.
- *Claude's gloss:* the treatment for the fastest-growing share of overdose death is contingency management, which is capped by anti-kickback law, and a medication combination with a small effect. Stimulant deaths without fentanyl rose in 2024 while opioid deaths fell. If the decline is a fentanyl story, this is the next wave, and it has no methadone.
**links:** methamphetamine · cocaine · contingency-management · the-decline · prohibition (pseudoephedrine control moved production, not use) · glp-1.

### overdose-prevention-centres
- OnPoint NYC opened the first two sanctioned centres in the US (East Harlem and Washington Heights, November 2021) and has reversed more than 1,600 overdoses.
- A JAMA Network Open study (del Pozo et al., 2023) found no significant change in crime or disorder around the sites, compared with 17 syringe-service programs elsewhere in the city.
- No overdose death occurred at the sites in the first evaluation period.
**links:** naloxone · prohibition (sites banned in Philadelphia and elsewhere).

### drug-checking
- Test strips exist for fentanyl, xylazine and medetomidine, and FTIR spectrometry reads the whole sample. New York's program has tested more than 3,000 samples since November 2021.
- Medetomidine and nitazene strips cost more and are less available. Some states still restrict distribution of test strips.
- *Claude's gloss:* the cheapest intervention on the information gap. A pill that is read before it is taken is no longer a random draw.
**links:** fentanyl · supply-substitution · mac-miller.

### syringe-services (Scott County, Indiana)
- Scott County, Indiana (population about 24,000), which rarely saw one new HIV case a year, had the largest HIV outbreak among people who inject drugs ever recorded in a non-urban US setting: 215 infections, driven by injection of the prescription opioid Opana (oxymorphone). Local health officials had warned the state years earlier of rising opioid injection and a hepatitis C outbreak, and recommended syringe programs; Indiana law prohibited them.
- Governor Mike Pence, who opposed needle exchange and said he would "pray on it", declared a public health emergency on 26 March 2015 and permitted a temporary syringe program. The local sheriff had urged it.
- Gonsalves and Crawford (Yale; Lancet HIV, 2018): undiagnosed infections peaked at about 126 around 10 January 2015, two months before the emergency was declared. A response in April 2011 could have limited the outbreak to 10 cases or fewer; one in January 2013 to about 56. A separate agent-based model (2019) estimated a proactive syringe program could have averted 90.3% of infections.
- In 2017 the CDC listed 220 other counties at risk of similar outbreaks. Smaller ones followed in Ohio, Kentucky, West Virginia, Massachusetts and Seattle.
- *Claude's gloss:* the syringe is the injection route's information gap: a clean one costs cents, and the infections it prevents cost a lifetime of treatment each. The delay was a moral position (syringe programs "enable" use) held against the evidence, and the evidence was a count of infections that could have been fewer than ten.
**links:** prohibition · purdue-pharma (prescription opioids injected) · naloxone · drug-checking · the-thesis.

### hepatitis-c (a cure withheld by price)
- Direct-acting antivirals, from 2013–2014 (sofosbuvir, launched by Gilead at $84,000 a course), cure more than 95% of hepatitis C infections in 8–12 weeks. Injection drug use is now the main route of transmission, and infections in the US rose about fourfold in a decade.
- To control cost, state Medicaid programs restricted the cure: to patients with advanced liver damage (fibrosis restrictions), to those abstinent from drugs and alcohol (sobriety restrictions, often six months) and to specialist prescribers. In 381,373 Medicaid recipients filling prescriptions from 2014 to 2021 (Furukawa et al., JAMA Network Open, 2024), fibrosis and sobriety restrictions and non-expansion of Medicaid were each associated with lower treatment rates.
- Louisiana's "Netflix model" (July 2019): a five-year subscription with Asegua (Gilead's subsidiary) for unlimited treatment at a capped annual cost, covering Medicaid patients and prisoners, with the sobriety and fibrosis restrictions removed. Prescriptions rose from 43.1 to 206.0 per 100,000 enrollees a quarter, a 534% relative increase (Auty et al., JAMA Health Forum, 2021). Washington's version, which had dropped its restrictions in 2016, saw little change.
- *Claude's gloss:* a sobriety rule for a curable infection withholds the cure from the people most likely to transmit it, which is the opposite of public health. The subscription model shows the price problem has a solution once the payer buys the cure for the population instead of the dose for the patient.
**links:** syringe-services · prisons · methadone and buprenorphine · the-thesis (evidence–practice gap) · purdue-pharma (price set by the holder).

### mortality-by-country
- Drug-related deaths per million (2024): the USA about 230; Scotland 191 (age-adjusted, Europe's highest for seven years running and 3.6 times its 2000 rate); Estonia 135 (2023); England and Wales 63, their highest since records began; the EU average about 25.
- Scotland's 2024 total was 1,017, down 13% but more than four times the 1996 level. Cocaine-involved deaths held at a record 479 for the second year.
- Glasgow opened The Thistle, the UK's first safer drug consumption facility, as a three-year pilot in January 2025. It was used 2,691 times in its first four months, with 30 medical emergencies, and registered 575 users in its first year. Seven in ten injections there are of cocaine.
- *Claude's gloss:* the US rate is nine times the EU average. The difference between countries with the same drugs available is the supply (fentanyl dominates in North America, heroin in most of Europe) and the treatment system.
**links:** overdose-prevention-centres · portugal · heroin-assisted-treatment · fentanyl · cocaine.

### global (the world drug report)
- UNODC's World Drug Report 2025 (26 June 2025): drug use at historically high levels, with global instability strengthening organised crime. Drug use disorders caused nearly half a million deaths and 28 million disability-adjusted life years in 2021. Only 26 of 110 reporting countries provide access to naloxone, and 76 of 133 offer some opioid agonist treatment (IDPC's reading of the report). In Africa only 3.4% of people with drug use disorders receive treatment.
- **Captagon** (fenethylline, in practice mostly amphetamine) is the most-used illicit drug in the Gulf states. Assad's government was widely accused of profiting from its production; after his fall large manufacturing sites were uncovered, and seizures in 2024–2025 show it still flowing to the Arabian peninsula.
- **Tramadol** misuse is the opioid crisis of West, Central and North Africa; Africa accounted for 57% of pharmaceutical opioids seized worldwide in 2019–2023. West Africa accounts for 70% of synthetic cannabinoid seizures, mostly "kush", a smokable blend that appeared there in the late 2010s. Methamphetamine keeps rising in South-East Asia, and cocaine production is at record levels, moving into new markets in Africa and Asia.
- The World Drug Report 2026 (18 June 2026) places the North American fentanyl peak in 2022–2023 and warns of nitazenes and orphines arriving elsewhere.
- *Claude's gloss:* the same pattern repeats in every region: a cheap synthetic replaces a plant-derived drug, its dose is unknown, and treatment reaches a small fraction of those who need it. The evidence-practice gap is global, and the gap is widest where the people are poorest.
**links:** mortality-by-country · supply-substitution · fentanyl · methamphetamine · prohibition · naloxone · war-on-drugs.

### portugal
- Portugal decriminalised personal possession of all drugs in July 2001 (Law 30/2000), up to 10 days' supply. Use remains an administrative offence, and people are referred to "dissuasion commissions" instead of courts. In the 1990s an estimated 100,000 people used heroin in a country of 10 million, and about half of HIV cases came from shared syringes.
- **The case that it worked.** HIV transmission by syringe fell sharply within a few years, and the prison population fell 16.5% between 2000 and 2008. A 2009 Cato report found that none of the predicted harms came to pass.
- **The case against.** Reporting from Porto (Washington Post, 2023) found visible drug use and encampments returning. Adult use reported at 12.8% in 2022 against 7.8% in 2001 (still below the European average), and overdoses rising 2019–2023. Critics also note that deaths were falling before 2001, so crediting decriminalisation alone overstates it.
- João Goulão, the policy's architect, attributes the recent problems to cuts in treatment funding, decentralised oversight and pandemic demand, not to decriminalisation itself.
- *Claude's gloss:* both sides' evidence holds within its premises. Decriminalisation plus funded treatment cut HIV and incarceration. Decriminalisation with the treatment funding withdrawn did not hold the gains. The variable that predicts outcomes is treatment capacity, which is also what Oregon lacked.
**links:** oregon-measure-110 · heroin-assisted-treatment · methadone and buprenorphine · war-on-drugs.

### oregon-measure-110 and portugal
- Oregon decriminalised possession in 2020 (Measure 110) and recriminalised it in 2024 (HB 4002).
- Portland State University's final report found little evidence that Measure 110 caused rising crime or overdose deaths; deaths were rising before it, with COVID and fentanyl.
- Brookings (Humphreys, 2024) found no evidence that access to treatment improved.
- Portugal decriminalised in 2001 alongside a large treatment and social-services system that Oregon did not build.
- *Claude's gloss:* decriminalisation without treatment capacity changes who is punished, not who is helped. Each side cites the half of the evidence that suits it.
**links:** war-on-drugs · contingency-management · methadone and buprenorphine.

### naloxone
- The FDA approved Narcan nasal spray (4 mg) for over-the-counter sale on 29 March 2023, the first opioid-treatment drug sold without a prescription. Nearly every state already let pharmacists dispense it under standing orders. Two doses often cost about $50.
- It reverses only opioid overdose, and fentanyl can need several doses. It does nothing for a stimulant overdose, and it cannot reverse the sedation from xylazine or medetomidine, which are not opioids.
- An agent gave it to Juice WRLD at Midway.
**links:** μ-opioid · juice-wrld · the-decline (one candidate cause) · supply-substitution (sedatives that naloxone cannot reverse).

### heroin-assisted-treatment and safer-supply
- **Switzerland** began prescribing pharmaceutical heroin (diacetylmorphine) under medical supervision in 1994. Germany, the Netherlands, Denmark, Belgium, Britain, Spain and Canada followed or tested it. In the canton of Zurich, new heroin users fell from 850 in 1990 to 150 in 2002.
- A 2023 systematic review of nine European and UK randomised trials found that heroin-assisted treatment more consistently retains people in treatment and reduces their use of illicit drugs.
- **Canada** opened North America's first heroin-assisted treatment programs in 2005, and safer-supply programs (prescribed hydromorphone and diacetylmorphine) in 2017. Studies in British Columbia found they reduce exposure to the toxic supply and reduce overdose deaths. Critics point to diversion of prescribed hydromorphone, and some provinces are now moving toward involuntary treatment.
- *Claude's gloss:* heroin-assisted treatment is the information gap closed by the state. The dose and the molecule are known, which is the opposite of the fentanyl market.
**links:** prohibition · drug-checking · methadone and buprenorphine · fentanyl.

### philippines
- Rodrigo Duterte's "war on drugs" began in 2016. The government acknowledges 6,248 killed in anti-drug operations; police records exceed 7,000; human-rights groups estimate up to 30,000, including killings by unidentified assailants later found to be police.
- He was arrested in Manila on 11 March 2025 on an ICC warrant for murder as a crime against humanity, covering November 2011 to March 2019 (as Davao mayor and as president), and flown to The Hague. He had withdrawn the Philippines from the ICC in 2019; the court holds jurisdiction for crimes committed while it was a member.
- The campaign had supermajority public support in 2017 and 2019 surveys. Killings continued through 2025.
- *Claude's gloss:* the endpoint of treating drug use as an enemy rather than a condition. The US war on drugs incarcerated; this one executed.
**links:** war-on-drugs · prohibition.

### alcohol and tobacco (the two largest drug killers)
- About 178,000 US deaths a year are from excessive alcohol use (2020–2021 average), up 29% from 137,927 in 2016–2017 (CDC MMWR, 2024). About 480,000 a year are from cigarette smoking and secondhand smoke.
- Both are legal, taxed and advertised. Tobacco kills more than any other drug in the US, and alcohol is second.
- The CDC's recommended policies for alcohol are higher taxes and fewer and less concentrated outlets: regulation of the supply and its price, not prohibition.
- *Claude's gloss:* the two deadliest drugs are legal and the comparison cuts both ways. Legal status does not make a drug safe, and its regulation (dose labelled, purity guaranteed, price and outlets controlled) is the tool the illicit market lacks. Prohibition of alcohol in the US (1920–1933) produced methanol poisoning and organised crime, the same information and supply failures as today.
**links:** prohibition · psilocybin (psilocybin for alcohol use disorder) · the-thesis.

### deaths-of-despair (overdose, suicide, alcohol as one category)
- Anne Case and Angus Deaton found in 2015 that all-cause mortality was rising among white non-Hispanic Americans aged 45–54, against the trend in every other rich country. Case named the three causes driving it, suicide, drug overdose and alcoholic liver disease, "deaths of despair". From 1999 to 2013 these three rose by 37 per 100,000 in that group.
- Their later work (The Great Divide, Annual Review of Economics, 2022; NBER 31236, 2023) makes the bachelor's degree the dividing line: adult life expectancy rises for those with a BA and has stalled or fallen for those without, and the US is the only rich country where the two move in opposite directions. For adults 25–74 without a BA from 2013 to 2019, the suicide rate rose 17% and drug mortality 73%. In the 2010s deaths of despair also began to rise among Black and Hispanic Americans without a BA, led by drugs. By 2025 they place the greatest risk in the youngest cohort, 25–34.
- Their causal account: pain and despair (the loss of good jobs, marriage, community and standing for those without a degree) created a baseline demand for opioids; Purdue and the industry supplied it.
- Critics (Mehta, Abrams and Myrskylä, 2020) argue that most of the US life-expectancy stall comes from the end of the decline in cardiovascular deaths, not from the rise in deaths of despair; and that "despair" is an inference about motive, since most overdoses are not intended. Case and Deaton reply that the cardiovascular stall is concentrated in the same less-educated population.
- *Claude's gloss:* both are true on their premises. Counted in deaths, heart disease is the larger share of the stall. Counted in years of life lost, and in the young, overdose and suicide dominate. "Despair" names a demand side; fentanyl is a supply side; the death rate is their product. A policy aimed at one factor leaves the other in place.
**links:** purdue-pharma · alcohol and tobacco · the-thesis · keltner (connection, meaning) · rat-park · native-nations · pain-patients.

### incentive-sensitization (the neuroscience of addiction)
- Robinson and Berridge, 1993; reviewed in the Annual Review of Psychology at 30 years in 2024. Mesolimbic dopamine mediates "wanting" (incentive salience), not "liking" (pleasure).
- Rats depleted of nearly all brain dopamine still showed normal "liking" reactions to sugar. Addiction is the sensitisation of wanting, triggered by cues, without a matching rise in liking.
- *Claude's gloss:* addiction is wanting without liking. Someone can crave what no longer brings pleasure, and that is why happiness (Keltner's measure) and reward are different quantities. A society optimised for wanting, which includes engagement-maximised media, is not optimised for happiness.
**links:** dopamine · keltner · contingency-management · brain-machine-interfaces (engagement as a non-drug supply of wanting).

### glp-1 (an addiction medicine found by accident)
- In a phase 2 randomised trial (Hendershot et al., JAMA Psychiatry, February 2025), low-dose semaglutide given weekly for nine weeks to 48 adults with alcohol use disorder who were not seeking treatment reduced the alcohol they drank in a laboratory test. It also reduced drinks per drinking day, heavy drinking days and weekly craving, though not the number of drinking days. Smokers in the trial cut cigarettes as well.
- Registry studies associate semaglutide with a 36% lower risk of hospitalisation for alcohol use disorder.
- *Claude's gloss:* a drug for weight acts on the wanting that incentive sensitization describes. It is the first new class with signal across alcohol, nicotine and possibly other drugs. It is small and early, and larger trials are the next step.
**links:** incentive-sensitization · alcohol and tobacco · dopamine.

### engagement (a non-drug supply of wanting)
- The debate: Jonathan Haidt (The Anxious Generation, 2024) argues that the move of adolescent social life onto smartphones and social media caused a rise in adolescent mental illness from about 2012, especially among girls.
- Candice Odgers (Nature review, 2024) argues the evidence is correlational, that analyses across many countries find no consistent association, and that earlier poor mental health may predict later social-media use rather than the reverse.
- Haidt's team (Rausch) cites longitudinal and experimental studies, including trials in which reducing social-media use improved wellbeing. They call it "a normal academic debate". US Surgeon General Vivek Murthy called for warning labels on social media.
- *Claude's gloss:* the premises differ in what counts as causal evidence. Odgers' holds that population correlations cannot establish cause. Haidt's holds that converging longitudinal, experimental and natural-experiment evidence can. The incentive-sensitization model and Meta's TRIBE encoding model sit underneath the question: platforms optimise for engagement, which is wanting, and wellbeing is a different quantity.
**links:** incentive-sensitization · brain-machine-interfaces (TRIBE) · keltner · dopamine.

### youth (counterfeit pills)
- Overdose deaths among US 14- to 18-year-olds more than doubled from 2019 to 2021 and reached 5.2 per 100,000, about 22 teenagers a week (Friedman, Hadland et al., JAMA 2022; NEJM research letter, January 2024). Over the same two decades drug use among 12th graders fell from about 21% to 8%. Rates are highest among Indigenous and Hispanic teenagers. Deaths among ages 0–19 were 1,622 in 2021, 1,590 in 2022 and 1,511 in 2023.
- The cause is counterfeit pills: tablets pressed to look like oxycodone (M30s), Xanax or Adderall that contain fentanyl. In 2022 the DEA found six in ten fentanyl-laced pills it tested held a potentially lethal dose. Teenagers looking for Xanax or Percocet bought them through social media, Snapchat most often; the National Crime Prevention Council estimates 80% of teen and young adult fentanyl deaths trace to some social-media contact.
- *Claude's gloss:* the teenagers did not use more drugs; the drug they used changed. This is the information gap at its most exact: a pill whose label is a lie, sold through a feed built for engagement. Drug-checking and honest drug education (what a counterfeit pill is and that naloxone exists) reach the risk; abstinence-only messaging does not, since it has nothing to say to the teenager who takes the pill anyway.
**links:** fentanyl · drug-checking · engagement (the platform as storefront) · naloxone · benzodiazepines · adderall · juice-wrld (Percocet) · lil-peep (fentanyl with Xanax).

### rat-park (environment and addiction)
- Bruce Alexander and colleagues at Simon Fraser University (1978–1981) built a colony 200 times the floor area of a standard cage. Rats living socially there drank less morphine solution than rats caged alone.
- Direct replications failed. One found that both caged and park rats preferred morphine less than in the original, and the author suggested a genetic cause. Isolation is not necessary for heroin or cocaine to be reinforcing.
- Conceptual replications hold. Social and environmental enrichment reduces drug self-administration across many later studies (Khoo 2020, "Have we reproduced Rat Park?"). The field's current model is a "three-hit" model: genes, early-life events and later environment.
- *Claude's gloss:* the popular version, that addiction is only about the cage, is overstated. The defensible version, that environment is one of three causes, carries policy weight: housing, community and purpose are treatment.
**links:** incentive-sensitization · oregon-measure-110 and portugal (social services) · keltner (connection as a health factor).

### housing-first (Rat Park as policy)
- Housing First, developed by Sam Tsemberis at Pathways to Housing in New York, gives people who are homeless with serious mental illness or addiction an apartment first, with no requirement of abstinence or treatment, and offers voluntary support afterward. In the original New York studies (2000–2004), 79% stayed housed at six months against 27% in the treatment-first system.
- Canada's At Home/Chez Soi (2008–2013, five cities, $110 million, the largest randomised trial of its kind): Housing First participants spent far more time stably housed and their quality of life improved faster.
- Systematic reviews: housing stability improves consistently; health, substance use and mortality outcomes are mixed, with "decidedly no negative impact" (Collins, University of Washington, 2025; Health Affairs brief, August 2026). Substance-related outcomes match treatment-first housing and are better than no housing. Critics point out that the first US trials found no greater improvement in substance use or psychiatric symptoms, and argue that requiring treatment would do better. A 2025 simulation (Rao and Brandeau, JAMA Network Open) models Housing First's effect on overdoses and mortality from opioid use disorder.
- The US counted 771,480 people homeless on a single night in January 2025.
- *Claude's gloss:* the critics' premise is that housing is a reward for recovery; the evidence is that housing does not cause relapse and does keep people alive and reachable. Housing First does not cure addiction by itself, and it was never designed to. It is the precondition for every other intervention here, since methadone, buprenorphine, contingency management and hepatitis C treatment all need an address.
**links:** rat-park · deaths-of-despair · methadone and buprenorphine · oregon-measure-110 and portugal · keltner.

### deep-brain-stimulation (addiction)
- Deep brain stimulation of the nucleus accumbens and ventral capsule for treatment-refractory opioid use disorder: a safety trial of 4 participants (J. Neurosurgery, 2024) had no serious adverse events, and a randomised sham-controlled trial is being started.
- A UK trial (ISRCTN78042015, recruiting since 2024) tests dual and single stimulation against sham for alcohol and opioid use disorder. A University of Colorado crossover trial (n = 5) tests it for methamphetamine use disorder.
- A 2024 perspective (Sakai et al., Frontiers in Psychiatry) calls the approach "promising" and "requiring caution".
**links:** brain-machine-interfaces · dopamine · docs/neural-co-processing.md (adaptive DBS as a closed-loop co-processor).

### sacred use (the medicines older than prohibition)
- **Peyote.** The Native American Church incorporated in Oklahoma in 1918 to protect its peyote sacrament. Federal regulations exempted its religious use after the Controlled Substances Act; Employment Division v. Smith (1990) held states could still prohibit it, and Congress then protected it by statute (1994).
- **Ayahuasca.** In Gonzales v. O Centro Espírita Beneficente União do Vegetal (21 February 2006), the Supreme Court ruled unanimously under the Religious Freedom Restoration Act that the UDV may use hoasca (ayahuasca, containing DMT) in worship. A second church, Santo Daime's Church of the Holy Light of the Queen, also has an exemption.
- **Bhang.** India's Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act (1985), enacted under international pressure, prohibits cannabis resin and flowers (charas, ganja) but excludes the leaves and seeds. So bhang, made from the leaves, remains legal, and some states license bhang shops. It is offered to Shiva and drunk at Holi and Maha Shivaratri.
- **Iboga.** The Bwiti tradition of Gabon uses the root of *Tabernanthe iboga* in initiation.
- *Claude's gloss:* each of these traditions holds the substance inside a structure of meaning, ceremony and community. Keltner's account of awe, and the clinical finding that psychedelic therapy works through guided experience, describe that structure in scientific terms.
**links:** dmt · iboga · cannabis · keltner (awe) · psilocybin · war-on-drugs.

### psychoplastogens (is the experience necessary?)
- David Olson (UC Davis Institute for Psychedelics and Neurotherapeutics; co-founder of Delix Therapeutics, which has raised more than $100 million) named "psychoplastogens": small molecules that rapidly promote structural plasticity in cortical neurons (neurite growth, dendritic spine density, synapse number). Psilocybin, LSD, DMT and ketamine are all psychoplastogens; the plasticity runs through the 5-HT2A receptor (and TrkB/BDNF downstream).
- In 2020 his lab described tabernanthalog (TBG), a non-hallucinogenic analogue of ibogaine that promoted plasticity and had antidepressant and anti-addictive effects in rodents. Later work (Aarrestad et al., 2025) reports that TBG promotes plasticity without the glutamate burst and immediate-early-gene activation that track hallucinogenic effects. A non-hallucinogenic neuroplastogen has reported rapid antidepressant effects in a phase 1b depression trial (company release, 2025).
- **Olson's case:** the evidence that the subjective experience causes the benefit is largely correlational (mystical-experience scores predict outcome, but that does not show the experience is the mechanism); a drug without the trip could be prescribed at scale, without hours of supervised sessions, and to patients excluded now (heart disease, psychosis risk).
- **Yaden and Griffiths' case:** across trials, the intensity of the acute experience (mystical and insight ratings) predicts lasting outcome; meaning-making is the therapy.
- *Claude's gloss:* both premises can be true at once: plasticity opens a window, and what is learned in the window decides the result. The experience is then one way to fill the window; guided therapy, community and ceremony are others. This is the same structure as sacred use and Rat Park: the molecule changes how plastic the system is, and the environment determines what it becomes. For hyperactivity, the question "is the experience necessary" is the question of whether recognition (the felt sense) or the weight change alone is understanding — language-is-trained-synesthesia.
**links:** iboga (tabernanthalog) · psilocybin · lsd · dmt · ketamine · 5-HT2A · sacred use · rat-park · language-is-trained-synesthesia.

### rebus (the entropic brain and relaxed priors)
- Carhart-Harris et al., "The entropic brain" (Frontiers in Human Neuroscience, 2014): psychedelics raise the entropy of spontaneous brain activity (the range of states it visits). They disintegrate the default-mode network (DMN), the network active in self-referential thought, and decouple it from the medial temporal lobes. Normal waking consciousness is proposed to sit just below criticality, with entropy suppressed.
- Carhart-Harris and Friston, "REBUS and the anarchic brain" (Pharmacological Reviews, 2019) joins this to the free-energy principle and predictive processing. The brain predicts its inputs from hierarchical priors; psychedelics, acting on 5-HT2A receptors on deep-layer pyramidal cells, relax the precision (weight) of high-level priors, so bottom-up signals carry more weight. Depression, addiction and OCD are modelled as pathologically overweighted priors; the drug lets them be revised. Their analogy is simulated annealing: heat flattens the energy landscape, and as the system cools it settles into new attractor basins.
- Critiques: entropy as a biomarker of the psychedelic state is oversimplified (different entropy measures disagree; results depend on preprocessing and on motion and physiological artefacts). The DMN findings come from small samples. An ethical analysis (Villiger and Trachsel) notes that the same relaxation makes a person more open to the beliefs of whoever guides them: suggestibility is part of the mechanism.
- *Claude's gloss:* REBUS and psychoplastogens are one model at two levels: relaxed priors at the level of inference, structural plasticity at the level of synapses, both describing a window in which the model can be rewritten. The ethical point closes the loop with sacred use and with engagement: whoever controls the setting during the window writes the new prior. That is the same power the TRIBE line of research measures for media. In hyperactivity's terms, a prior is a world model, and annealing it is understanding revised by recognition rather than by words.
**links:** psychoplastogens · 5-HT2A · psilocybin · lsd · dmt · sacred use · engagement · brain-machine-interfaces (TRIBE) · language-is-trained-synesthesia.

